{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>But if on the morrow, and that which remains of it shall be eaten</b> — This is an extraneous vav, and there are many like it in Scripture, for example, \"And these are the sons of Zibeon: both Aiah and Anah\" (Genesis 36:24), and likewise \"to give both the holy place and the host to be trampled underfoot\" (Daniel 8:13) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 7:16). And so Ibn Ezra explained as well. But in my opinion, the purport of this verse is that since it says, \"on the day of his offering his sacrifice shall be eaten, and on the morrow,\" it might have been understood that there is a commandment to eat it over two days — to eat part of it on the first day and leave part of it for the next day. Therefore it went back to explain: \"and that which remains of it — on the first day it shall be eaten, and on the morrow,\" meaning not that he should intentionally leave part of it over, nor that he is permitted to leave all of it for the next day, but rather it is a commandment to eat of it on the first day, and whatever remains incidentally may be eaten on the aforementioned morrow. This idea we learned from the words of our Sages, for they said in Torat Kohanim (Sifra, chapter 12:11–12): \"'On the day of his offering it shall be eaten' — this is a commandment to eat of it on the first day. One might think all of it — Scripture says, 'and on the morrow.' One might think it is a commandment to eat it over two days — Scripture says, 'and that which remains,' meaning if he left some over, he left some over. But if 'and that which remains' — one might think that if he left all of it over for two days it would be disqualified — Scripture says, 'it shall be eaten,' even all of it.\" And if you take the vav as extraneous, it could also be explained as: \"and on the morrow, that which remains of it shall be eaten\" — not that he should intentionally leave it over. And I do not know why the vav in \"to give both the holy place and the host to be trampled underfoot\" would be extraneous according to Rashi, for the meaning of that verse is asking: until when will the transgression continue, desolating, and until when will the holy place and the host be trampled underfoot?"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "Ramban questions Rashi's characterization of the vav in Daniel 8:13 as extraneous, arguing it functions normally in a compound question."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:30.705453319Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}